WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.110
Ms. Marvel's about to "embiggen" her way to
a wider audience, and the first trailer for

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:07.290
the new show has plenty to discuss.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:08.900
Is she still an Inhuman?

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.920
Where'd she get those cosmic powers?

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.660
And how does Captain Marvel herself fit into
all this?

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.949
If you're watching the "Ms. Marvel" trailer
without knowledge of the comics, one of the

00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:23.130
biggest things that'll catch your eye is Kamala
Khan's glowing purple energy construct powers.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:27.800
Comic fans, though, will immediately recognize
that Ms. Marvel's abilities are vastly different.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.870
Kamala — who first debuted back in "Captain
Marvel" Vol. 7 #14 before headlining her own

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:38.120
series in 2014 — was originally depicted
as getting her powers when the mutation-causing

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.100
Terrigen Mist, which are traditionally associated
with the Inhumans, rolls through Jersey City.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:47.590
Now, quick history lesson for casual fans:
those aforementioned Inhumans use Terrigen

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:52.390
Crystals to awaken their dormant abilities,
which themselves are the product of Kree experimentation

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.100
from thousands of years ago.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.460
That means, yes, Kamala Khan is an Inhuman
in the comics, and after exposure to the mist,

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:02.750
she gets what she calls "embiggening" power
where she can stretch and grow her limbs to

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:03.870
any size.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:08.439
This makes her a polymorph, capable of expanding,
contracting, or reshaping her body in various

00:01:08.439 --> 00:01:11.640
ways, similar to Reed Richards of the Fantastic
Four.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.009
Evidently, that won't be the case in the MCU.

00:01:14.009 --> 00:01:18.360
The new trailer confirms that Kamala now has
"cosmic" powers, which allow her to create

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.950
various energy constructs, seemingly based
on her imagination.

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:26.920
At one pivotal moment she manifests a giant,
shimmering energy fist — so the essence

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:31.100
of her embiggening powers will still be there,
but reshaped for the MCU.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.100
Why the change?

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.950
It's probably a combination of the "Inhumans"
TV series being a flop, and that a "Fantastic

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:40.690
Four" movie is currently in the works from
Jon Watts — so, presumably, Marvel doesn't

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:44.659
want to have both Kamala and Reed's powersets
look too similar.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:49.450
About halfway through the trailer, we watch
as Kamala attaches a pair of glowing bracelets

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:52.500
to her wrists, which causes her eyes to glow
bright purple.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.189
These are most likely the Nega Bands: powerful
artifacts that have been used by various characters

00:01:57.189 --> 00:02:01.289
throughout comic book history, but traditionally
are depicted as having a unique connection

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:03.399
to Captain Marvel and the Kree.

00:02:03.399 --> 00:02:07.470
In the comics, these bands were entrusted
by the Kree Supreme Intelligence to the original

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.740
Captain Marvel, a Kree warrior named Mar-Vell.

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:15.250
A reimagined form of this character was played
by Annette Bening in 2019's "Captain Marvel."

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:19.640
The Nega-Bands have the ability to convert
mental energy into physical energy.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:24.360
In the comics, that's meant that the various
Captains Marvel can shoot energy beams, gain

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:28.420
super strength, and are gifted with an ambiguously
defined "cosmic awareness."

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:32.580
In the show, it looks like they'll function
similarly to a Green Lantern ring.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:36.670
Either way, whoever wears the Nega-Bands is
able to wield an immense amount of power,

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:40.500
and the fact that Kamala's eyes glow after
she puts them on seems to imply that she gains

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.040
her powers after wearing them for the first
time.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.620
That said, the fact that the show's Nega-Bands'
power comes from mental energy implies that

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:51.970
whoever wields them must have a very active
imagination — which, as the trailer makes

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:55.380
clear, is a description that very aptly applies
to Kamala.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:59.690
The trailer constantly reiterates how Kamala
is often living in her own "little fantasy

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:03.360
land," whether it's through her artwork or
her love for superheroes.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.410
While the adults around her appear to be treating
her imagination as a flaw, it may be that

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:11.980
it's exactly that trait which allows her to
harness the full power of the Nega-Bands.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:15.920
After all, as we've recently seen in "Shang-Chi
and the Legend of the Ten Rings," it takes

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.040
more than a pair of mystical bands to make
a true hero.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.870
"I'm a superhero."

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:26.230
You don't have to dig too deep in the history
of Marvel Comics to find the impact Ms. Marvel

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:27.870
had on the industry.

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:32.380
Kamala Khan was the first mainstream Muslim
superhero to headline her own book: The character's

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:37.090
debut was written about by outlets such as
NPR, as her Muslim heritage changed the face

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:38.170
of comics.

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:41.930
That makes Kamala's religion, her cultural
background, and everything connected to it

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:45.870
essential to the character, and one would
hope that the same will hold true for her

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:47.410
live-action counterpart.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:51.800
Kamala's name is part of that, and many readers
have often been not entirely clear on the

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:55.950
correct pronunciation of it, particularly
since it differs from the pronunciation used

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:58.890
by current U.S. Vice President Kamala Harris.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:03.000
Thankfully, Marvel Studios and Disney+ address
that immediately in the new trailer for "Ms.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.060
Marvel" with a quick moment where she corrects
a schoolmate on the pronunciation of her name:

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:09.060
"Comma-la."

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.060
"Kah-mah-lah."

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:11.510
"Another Avengers shirt.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:12.510
Cute."

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:15.440
Even better, though, is that this isn't the
only nod to Khan's heritage.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.489
The trailer also shows a quick shot of her
in a mosque, wearing a hijab, and performing

00:04:19.489 --> 00:04:21.900
the Muslim prayer known as salah.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:25.290
These moments in the trailer show that her
faith and heritage won't be disappearing as

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.430
the character makes her way from the comic
book pages to live-action.

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:33.650
Throughout the wide array of Marvel movies
and TV shows that we've been lucky enough

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:37.720
to see over the years, there have been a few
rare instances where shots taken directly

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.660
from the comics are captured perfectly on
the screen.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:44.560
For instance, it's hard to forget that "Spider-Man
2" shot of Spider-Man's costume being dumped

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:50.220
into a trash can, taken right from Amazing
Spider-Man #50, or the moment in "Black Panther"

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.890
where Killmonger throws T'Challa over the
cliff.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:56.930
Well, judging by the final shot in this new
trailer, "Ms. Marvel" is getting the same

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:57.930
treatment.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:01.730
That stunning sight of this all-new hero,
overlooking the big city as she sits casually

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:06.340
on top of a streetlamp, comes directly from
this 2014 comic cover.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:10.190
Fittingly enough, this moment also shows that
while much of the series may feature Kamala

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:14.120
in a makeshift Captain Marvel outfit, she
will, at one point or another, get to don

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:17.230
her own iconic threads before the series ends.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:21.730
We can't wait to see her start this journey
when "Ms. Marvel" arrives on Disney+ on June 8th. 

